[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1119,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 1158,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154946,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 41,
        "headingId": 1158,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lipofibromatosis (LPF) is an intermediate, locally aggressive paediatric soft tissue neoplasm with a predilection for the hands and feet; it is composed of an admixture of mature fat, short fascicles of bland spindle cells, and lipoblast-like cells in the interface between these components.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T15:00:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1120,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 1159,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154947,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 41,
        "headingId": 1159,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8851/1 Lipofibromatosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T15:00:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1121,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 1160,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154948,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 41,
        "headingId": 1160,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>FB51.Y &amp; XH4QB6 Other specified fibroblastic disorders &amp; Lipofibromatosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T15:00:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1122,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 1161,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154949,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 41,
        "headingId": 1161,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> infantile/juvenile fibromatosis variant (non-desmoid type).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T15:00:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1123,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 1162,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154950,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 41,
        "headingId": 1162,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T15:00:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1124,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1163,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154951,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 41,
        "headingId": 1163,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LPF preferentially involves the hands and feet, although it can also occur at other locations such as the trunk and the head and neck {11075850}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T15:00:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1125,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1164,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154952,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 41,
        "headingId": 1164,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LPF typically presents as a slow-growing, poorly demarcated subcutaneous mass, which can also involve skeletal muscle {11075850}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T15:00:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1126,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1165,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154953,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 41,
        "headingId": 1165,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LPF is rare and occurs more often in boys, with a wide age range from birth to 14&nbsp;years. Half of the cases are diagnosed by the age of 1&nbsp;year, and as many as 20% of reported cases have been congenital {11075850; 30310176}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T15:00:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1127,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1166,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154954,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 41,
        "headingId": 1166,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T15:00:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1128,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1167,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154955,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 41,
        "headingId": 1167,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LPF may represent a morphological pattern common to a variety of locally recurring paediatric tumours of the extremities, rather than to a single specific entity. The presence of <em>FN1</em>::<em>EGF</em> or <em>FN1</em>::<em>TGFA</em> fusions suggests that some cases probably represent early or non-calcified calcifying aponeurotic fibromas. Moreover, identification of fusions involving genes encoding EGFR (HER1) ligands (<em>EGF</em>, <em>HBEGF</em>, <em>TGFA</em>) or <em>EGFR</em> itself, or genes encoding other receptor tyrosine kinases (<em>ROS1</em>, <em>RET</em>, <em>PDGFRB</em>), suggests that activation of the PI3K/AKT/mTOR pathway might be implicated in the pathogenesis of LPF {30310176}.</p>\r\n<p>Cytogenetic abnormalities have been described in 1&nbsp;case {18036401}, and molecular analyses have identified fusions involving <em>EGF</em> or other genes encoding receptor tyrosine kinases or their ligands in a subset of LPF cases {30310176}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T15:00:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1135,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1174,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154956,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 41,
        "headingId": 1174,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The firm, rubbery, poorly demarcated mass has a white or yellow cut surface. Most are 10&ndash;70&nbsp;mm in diameter, although very large tumours have been reported involving the entire extremity {11075850; 30310176; 19461290}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T15:00:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1129,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1168,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154957,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 41,
        "headingId": 1168,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lipofibromatosis is composed of mature adipose tissue traversed by cellular fascicles of spindle cells with bland, uniform, elongated nuclei. Fat usually forms a major proportion of the lesion. In newborn patients, the fat lobules may have an immature appearance with myxoid matrix. Small collections of univacuolated cells may be present in the fibroblastic component, where melanin-laden cells have occasionally been documented. Lipofibromatosis entraps subcutaneous tissue and sometimes skeletal muscle. Mitotic activity is rare and necrosis absent. Occasional recurrent lesions are indistinguishable from calcifying aponeurotic fibroma {30310176}. By immunohistochemistry, the fibroblasts show variable expression of CD34 and SMA. The spindle cells are negative for desmin, in contrast to the spindle cells in lipoblastoma. The uncommon pigmented cells express melanocytic markers {11075850; 18236073}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T15:00:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1130,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1169,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154958,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 41,
        "headingId": 1169,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Insufficient data</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T15:00:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1131,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
